record docwrite.observation.reader-clipboard-arboard/1 : observation {
    title "Reader clipboard runs on arboard with Wayland support"
    state verified
    statement """
        The reader's clipboard backend moved from copypasta 0.10 (X11-only ClipboardContext on Linux) to arboard 3.6 with default-features off plus wayland-data-control: arboard tries Wayland when WAYLAND_DISPLAY is set and falls back to X11, both round-tripped live, and copypasta is fully out of the lockfile. The lazy reconnect, external wl-copy/xclip/xsel fallback (non-blocking: holders never exit, so success is 'accepted stdin' with a reaper thread), status-bar notice, and stub-script tests are unchanged; no from-scratch crate was needed.
        """
    observed_at git:bfe8bd07dfb6dc568fe34fb51f466dc8f74f0dc7
    method observation
    watches [ "crates/liminal-reader/src/ui.rs", "Cargo.toml", "Cargo.lock" ]
    author "dk"
}

record docwrite.observation.reader-clipboard-needs-external-fallback/1 : observation {
    title "Reader clipboard needs external-tool fallback on Wayland"
    state verified
    statement """
        copypasta 0.10's ClipboardContext is X11-only on Linux (wayland_clipboard needs an external wl_display winit does not expose), so on a Wayland-only session SystemClipboard::new() failed once at startup and every copy/paste silently no-oped with only a stderr line — which read as 'cannot copy from Markdown'. ui.rs now connects lazily with reconnect-on-failure, falls back to wl-copy/wl-paste then xclip/xsel, and surfaces a persistent failure in the status bar. The Markdown substrate-to-text leg itself was intact (pinned by markdown_selection_copies_through_the_search_index).
        """
    observed_at git:bfe8bd07dfb6dc568fe34fb51f466dc8f74f0dc7
    method observation
    watches [ "crates/liminal-reader/src/ui.rs", "Cargo.lock" ]
    author "dk"
}

record docwrite.observation.reader-open-sniffs-content-first/1 : observation {
    title "Reader open classifies by content before extension"
    state verified
    statement """
        Document opening routed purely by extension at three gates (liminaldoc router, reader cli::is_markdown_path, app::open_document_engine), so a PDF with a wrong/missing suffix failed and text misnamed .pdf died in the PDF engine. Opening now classifies by content first (cli::classify_document_path: %PDF- magic scan, then text sniff, then extension), the launcher sniffs PDF magic before its extension check, and unknown binary keeps the PDF engine's honest parse error instead of rendering mojibake.
        """
    observed_at git:bfe8bd07dfb6dc568fe34fb51f466dc8f74f0dc7
    method observation
    watches [
        "crates/liminal-reader/src/cli.rs",
        "crates/liminal-reader/src/app.rs",
        "crates/liminaldoc/src/main.rs"
    ]
    author "dk"
}

record docwrite.observation.reader-resize-presentation/1 : observation {
    title "Reader resize: per-event presentation churn fixed; GPU surface still goes Outdated on X11 and falls back"
    state verified
    scope [ path "crates/liminal-reader/**" ]
    statement """
        Reader resize on X11 (Lubuntu LXQt, NVIDIA Vulkan). REPORTED by the user, not measured: dragging the border flashes through intermediate sizes, and sometimes the content trails the border very slowly. FOUND BY CODE READING (a Fable advisor agreed independently): WindowEvent::Resized ran a full resize, compose and present per event, and GpuCompositor::resize reconfigures the swapchain unconditionally under AutoVsync, so a burst of border-drag events queued blocking presents; the window also opened with PhysicalSize rather than LogicalSize, so on a scaled display it opened smaller and could shrink further than intended. CHANGED: Resized now only lays out and requests a redraw (winit folds the burst into one RedrawRequested per loop turn); the synchronous present stays only under cfg(windows) for the modal resize loop, untested; presenters adopt the scene size lazily on the frame actually drawn, with WgpuPresenter skipping same-size resizes; ScaleFactorChanged requests a redraw; window sizes are logical; the reader window and dialog title is now one WINDOW_TITLE constant, 'Liminaldoc', with the document name after an em dash. NOT FIXED, and the likely cause of the 'sometimes very slow' symptom: under a fast resize burst the wgpu surface returns Outdated, lege-gpu reconfigures at its stale stored size, the second acquire fails, and present_scene permanently falls back to the softbuffer presenter (full-window CPU repaint every frame). Instrumented: in a failing run the scene, swapchain configuration and XGetGeometry size all agreed at 708x504 after the burst settled, yet 8 immediate reconfigure-draw-present attempts all returned SurfaceLost. UNTESTED hypothesis: the driver's surface extent lags the X window by some milliseconds, so retries inside that gap all fail; a short sleep between attempts, or a deferred retry driven by about_to_wait, would test it (the sleep experiment was not run). The fallback is permanent for the process and not retried. Whether the original code fell back as often was not measured. No visual flicker or lag measurement was taken at any stage; see the stress evidence for rates.
        """
    observed_at git:74677babc582e3f94ab342808abacf526e271729
    method instrumented
    watches [ "crates/liminal-reader/src/app.rs", "crates/liminal-reader/src/present/**" ]
    verified_by [
        @docwrite.evidence.reader-resize-checks,
        @docwrite.evidence.reader-resize-x11-stress
    ]
    author "dk"
}

record docwrite.observation.viewer-paging-is-line-continuity/1 : observation {
    title "lege-viewer pages by line continuity and snaps only on command"
    state superseded
    statement """
        lege-viewer's PageUp/PageDown is line-continuity paging: paging_target makes the last
        fully visible line the new top line, with notional-line and 8%-overlap geometric
        fallbacks (lege-viewer/src/scroll/paging.rs). Nearest-page snap is an explicit command
        (nearest_page_boundary, bound to S) and never happens automatically. Reusable, not
        PDF-coupled: scroll/anchor.rs, frame.rs, damage.rs, diagnostics.rs, trace.rs, scene.rs,
        present/*. The conductor/tile pipeline and the Pdf-variant artifact enums in
        document/engine.rs assume read-only content.
        """
    observed_at git:823d46ddc946bb5d6ac8fc360a9d9ba6506d7a7d
    method manual
    watches [ "../lege-viewer/src/scroll/**", "../lege-viewer/src/document/engine.rs" ]
}

